  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA trough over the central Plains will begin to eject east today\nas the trough lifts into the OH Valley during the day on Friday.\nA ridge will be present over the southeast/eastern CONUS as this\nwill slowly impede and weaken the front as it approaches. In\nfact, the northern end of the rain shield the ensembles are\nadvertising seems to shear out and dissipate the precipitation a\nbit. The NBM shows roughly 50% to 70% of exceeding 0.25\" over in\nour Ohio counties but its more around 30% to 50% for the PA and\nWV counties. This makes sense as the front seems to deteriorate\nby the time it begins to push into PA. \n\nThat said, the probs for SB CAPE to even exceed 250 J/Kg is only\n30% to 50% and this area is southeast of Pittsburgh. The other\nfactor is that the SPC only has a general thunder area for the\narea south of I-70. The WPC does not have a marginal outlook for\nthe area for Friday either. Thus no impacts are expected,\nalthough, the 850 MB winds advertise a 30 to 40 knot LL Jet.\nThis might be enough to get a few wind gusts of 20 to 30 knots\nthrough the day today but that would be the extent. Also the NBM\nprobs give a 30% to 50% probability for wind gusts above 25 mph\nand even higher on the ridges. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate broad upper troughing should persist \nacross the NE CONUS from the weekend into the middle of next \nweek. A southern stream shortwave could bring a few showers to \nareas south and east of Pittsburgh on Sunday. This disturbance would\nbe followed by an influx of cooler air into the region, dropping\ntemperatures below climatology early next week while another run\nof dry weather begins thanks to high pressure.\n\nOne thing to watch will be the potential for frost both Tuesday and\nWednesday mornings to the northeast of Pittsburgh, as well as \nin the usual Laurel Highland/northern West Virginia cold spots. \nThe NBM paints a 50% to 75% chance of low temperatures colder \nthan 37 degrees north of US-422 both mornings - which would be \nchilly enough for at least patchy frost given clear and calm \novernight conditions. Temperatures from Wednesday on should \nbuild back towards more seasonal values.",
